totype/commits/fd31ffa416f270f8d65189ffd7e8c2439853ef69"}],"sha":"f3f79e3bd3f0b82f61ad2cf25035910fe2ccbece","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/codebend3r/micro-fe-prototype/commits/f3f79e3bd3f0b82f61ad2cf25035910fe2ccbece"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1402814?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/codebend3r/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/codebend3r/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/codebend3r/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/codebend3r/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/codebend3r","id":1402814,"login":"codebend3r","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE0MDI4MTQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/codebend3r/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/codebend3r/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/codebend3r/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/codebend3r/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/codebend3r/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/codebend3r","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/codebend3r/micro-fe-prototype/commits/fd31ffa416f270f8d65189ffd7e8c2439853ef69/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-14T18:12:31Z","email":"cj.rivas.dev@gmail.com","name":"CJ Rivas"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-14T18:12:31Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Micro frontend integration strategies, compared side by side (#1)\n\n* Add both micro frontend integration strategies with a live comparison harness\n\nBuilds two complete Shell plus app1 plus app2 systems on Vite and\n@module-federation/vite, differing only in whether React is shared, and a\nharness that runs both at once so they can be compared directly.\n\n* Option 1 (`option1-shared/`, ports 5010 to 5012) shares `react`,\n  `react-dom`, `@mfe/session` and `@mfe/shared-core` as singletons. Remotes\n  expose React components the Shell loads with `lazy(() => import(''app1/App''))`.\n* Option 2 (`option2-independent/`, ports 5020 to 5022) shares nothing. app1\n  ships React 18, app2 ships React 19, and remotes expose\n  `mount(el, props) => unmount` that the Shell drives from `RemoteMount`.\n* `compare/` on port 5000 runs both systems in iframes, drives them in\n  lockstep over `postMessage`, and renders a live table of the differences.\n* `packages/shared-core` holds the framework agnostic store, event bus and a\n  probe pinned to `globalThis` that counts distinct React instances by\n  `createElement` identity rather than version string.\n* `packages/session` is the React binding, and the single file where context\n  either does or does not cross an app boundary.\n* `packages/ui` carries the stylesheet, the shell hooks and the probe panel,\n  shared by both Shells so the two sides are measured by identical code.\n\nMeasured with both systems fully loaded: Option 1 runs 1 React copy and\n183.6 kB of JavaScript with remotes reading shell state through context;\nOption 2 runs 3 React copies (19.2.8 plus 18.3.1) and 254.6 kB with remotes\nreading it through mount props. A render error thrown in a remote is caught by\nthe Shell boundary in Option 1 and by the remote''s own boundary in Option 2.\n\n* Fail fast on port conflicts and document running and deploying each option\n\n`pnpm start` pins every port with `strictPort: true`, so a single leftover\nserver turned startup into seven simultaneous SIGTERM failures with no\nindication of the cause. Three fixes, plus the docs that were asked for.\n\n* `scripts/ports.mjs` runs before anything binds. It reports exactly which\n  port is taken and which process holds it, then points at `pnpm stop`. Every\n  start, dev and serve script calls it first, and the serve scripts also check\n  that a build exists rather than silently serving 404s.\n* `pnpm stop` frees the prototype''s ports, killing only `node` listeners so it\n  cannot take out an unrelated service.\n* The comparison harness moves from port 5000 to 5100. On macOS, AirPlay\n  Receiver listens on 5000 by default.\n* Adds `start:option1`, `start:option2`, `dev:option1`, `dev:option2`,\n  `build:option1` and `build:option2` so either stack runs on its own.\n* `scripts/vite-quiet.ts` filters the two harmless build warnings that read\n  like errors: `@module-federation/vite` importing `node:vm` in an SSR helper\n  the browser build never reaches, and Rollup reporting empty placeholder\n  chunks for shared modules that resolve to the host''s singleton.\n\nThe README now gives exact commands for running each option alone (including\nthe three terminal equivalent), a full script and port reference, a\ntroubleshooting section, and a deployment section covering static asset\nhosting, build time versus runtime remote resolution, and what each option''s\npipeline has to enforce.\n\n* Drop the commerce framing and label every control with what it demonstrates\n\nThe demo screens read as a shopping cart: parts with dollar prices, an \"Add\"\nbutton, an order total. That mislabels the one thing the buttons are actually\nfor, which is showing a value cross an app boundary, and it invites the\nquestion of what is being charged. Nothing was; the prices were invented.\n\n* Removes prices and `formatMoney` entirely. `CATALOG` rows are now name, sku\n  and category, and the store''s `order` becomes `selection`, with\n  `addToSelection`, `removeFromSelection` and `clearSelection`.\n* Renames the routes and tabs after the role each remote plays:\n  `Catalog (app1)` writes the shared state, `Selection (app2)` reads it. Each\n  panel header now says so directly.\n* Relabels the buttons after their effect. `Add` becomes `Add to shared\n  state`, `Throw a render error` becomes `Crash app1` / `Crash app2`, and the\n  Shell header chip reads `shared state N items` instead of an order total.\n* Gives every interactive group a short explainer above it saying what to\n  watch and why the two options reach the same result differently.\n* Replaces the product card grid with a row list, so the action button has\n  room for a label that is a sentence rather than a verb.\n\nVerified end to end after the rename: Option 1 still runs 1 React copy with\nremotes on context, Option 2 still runs 3 copies (19.2.8 plus 18.3.1) with\nremotes on mount props, and a crash is still caught by the Shell boundary in\nOption 1 and by the remote''s own boundary in Option 2.","tree":{"sha":"a77ff21265befa1c77a2f0e69d7f246cf4ab9334","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/codebend3r/micro-fe-prototype/git/trees/a77ff21265befa1c77a2f0e69d7f246cf4ab9334"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/codebend3r/micro-fe-prototype/git/commits/fd31ffa416f270f8d65189ffd7e8c2439853ef69","verification":{"payload":"tree a77ff21265befa1c77a2f0e69d7f246cf4ab9334\nparent 352fd0fa175cb8e3854a9b85aae9e754ef4ba4b5\nauthor CJ Rivas <cj.rivas.dev@gmail.com> 1786731151 -0400\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786731151 -0400\n\nMicro frontend integration strategies, compared side by side (#1)\n\n* Add both micro frontend integration strategies with a live comparison harness\n\nBuilds two complete Shell plus app1 plus app2 systems on Vite and\n@module-federation/vite, differing only in whether React is shared, and a\nharness that runs both at once so they can be compared directly.\n\n* Option 1 (`option1-shared/`, ports 5010 to 5012) shares `react`,\n  `react-dom`, `@mfe/session` and `@mfe/shared-core` as singletons. Remotes\n  expose React components the Shell loads with `lazy(() => import(''app1/App''))`.\n* Option 2 (`option2-independent/`, ports 5020 to 5022) shares nothing. app1\n  ships React 18, app2 ships React 19, and remotes expose\n  `mount(el, props) => unmount` that the Shell drives from `RemoteMount`.\n* `compare/` on port 5000 runs both systems in iframes, drives them in\n  lockstep over `postMessage`, and renders a live table of the differences.\n* `packages/shared-core` holds the framework agnostic store, event bus and a\n  probe pinned to `globalThis` that counts distinct React instances by\n  `createElement` identity rather than version string.\n* `packages/session` is the React binding, and the single file where context\n  either does or does not cross an app boundary.\n* `packages/ui` carries the stylesheet, the shell hooks and the probe panel,\n  shared by both Shells so the two sides are measured by identical code.\n\nMeasured with both systems fully loaded: Option 1 runs 1 React copy and\n183.6 kB of JavaScript with remotes reading shell state through context;\nOption 2 runs 3 React copies (19.2.8 plus 18.3.1) and 254.6 kB with remotes\nreading it through mount props. A render error thrown in a remote is caught by\nthe Shell boundary in Option 1 and by the remote''s own boundary in Option 2.\n\n* Fail fast on port conflicts and document running and deploying each option\n\n`pnpm start` pins every port with `strictPort: true`, so a single leftover\nserver turned startup into seven simultaneous SIGTERM failures with no\nindication of the cause. Three fixes, plus the docs that were asked for.\n\n* `scripts/ports.mjs` runs before anything binds. It reports exactly which\n  port is taken and which process holds it, then points at `pnpm stop`. Every\n  start, dev and serve script calls it first, and the serve scripts also check\n  that a build exists rather than silently serving 404s.\n* `pnpm stop` frees the prototype''s ports, killing only `node` listeners so it\n  cannot take out an unrelated service.\n* The comparison harness moves from port 5000 to 5100. On macOS, AirPlay\n  Receiver listens on 5000 by default.\n* Adds `start:option1`, `start:option2`, `dev:option1`, `dev:option2`,\n  `build:option1` and `build:option2` so either stack runs on its own.\n* `scripts/vite-quiet.ts` filters the two harmless build warnings that read\n  like errors: `@module-federation/vite` importing `node:vm` in an SSR helper\n  the browser build never reaches, and Rollup reporting empty placeholder\n  chunks for shared modules that resolve to the host''s singleton.\n\nThe README now gives exact commands for running each option alone (including\nthe three terminal equivalent), a full script and port reference, a\ntroubleshooting section, and a deployment section covering static asset\nhosting, build time versus runtime remote resolution, and what each option''s\npipeline has to enforce.\n\n* Drop the commerce framing and label every control with what it demonstrates\n\nThe demo screens read as a shopping cart: parts with dollar prices, an \"Add\"\nbutton, an order total. That mislabels the one thing the buttons are actually\nfor, which is showing a value cross an app boundary, and it invites the\nquestion of what is being charged. Nothing was; the prices were invented.\n\n* Removes prices and `formatMoney` entirely. `CATALOG` rows are now name, sku\n  and category, and the store''s `order` becomes `selection`, with\n  `addToSelection`, `removeFromSelection` and `clearSelection`.\n* Renames the routes and tabs after the role each remote plays:\n  `Catalog (app1)` writes the shared state, `Selection (app2)` reads it. Each\n  panel header now says so directly.\n* Relabels the buttons after their effect. `Add` becomes `Add to shared\n  state`, `Throw a render error` becomes `Crash app1` / `Crash app2`, and the\n  Shell header chip reads `shared state N items` instead of an order total.\n* Gives every interactive group a short explainer above it saying what to\n  watch and why the two options reach the same result differently.\n* Replaces the product card grid with a row list, so the action button has\n  room for a label that is a sentence rather than a verb.\n\nVerified end to end after the rename: Option 1 still runs 1 React copy with\nremotes on context, Option 2 still runs 3 copies (19.2.8 plus 18.3.1) with\nremotes on mount props, and a crash is still caught by the Shell boundary in\nOption 1 and by the remote''s own boundary in Option 2.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqf1qPCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAr9wQAIwQ6k7U1tMbJxUSzwwNdec3\n9eH6Lolf+cKVRVWNrJ/DA7LjC03BOmX/WfwXNoRns2Pu6+b+f0R7RTfqCQgwbjdd\nRuynPP3Bn8tzQWqEwUQ2FqKuuUWiMvd+0Rs6GBGqXDklVIIuHTIIkr2pJwrkrIc2\n2linNyEx+GpSK6APpd07zg+/hTu7PcTEsGMXvzHbAb40eFJODFp4Goss3fLOjtDc\nSvVf3hBIr1A3IwJCCHhzLBfd6A37OaRjoiC+dI8/SfrdVM9hufQ+NWMeeVQAYbA9\n1hHiLdZAO9F+wKDoyi4TrOK2uD0BvLO3MAvLM/ewOzExQcI0yKV9SSMGxGuDcwts\n8YKa9bubDgzLqH4QpkkaBO6+UoRe5yeoN4YiFFFkglvbQ/NxaQmbbleZ1orMVeG4\nMgYHtCcDxUCbyXiL24jCZsnzOha98GVRBGhQbtbYiWqiR62FwDPFg91aEsOrKd+L\n1+4pnwGF4WWXMV0y6XVgEUXLASAZlRIHRd1+v8C7JlI6niS8cZ7VETj+EUnvPpkE\nbbVPH3Bkl72K04tmyQ2LdDeDfQKXzxKAGg6cJX43J5HneDb+AYI+4kgn47c0eL0F\nX+4nmF5IcQ/1AKJiBoS2kHKHCoGd+VhAy3rSC2U/2DT8b+jRA6sV6WKSeyV/EMWJ\n9Y1EU6xBHxBF2l4yGzvS\n=gpV9\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-14T18:12:33Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/codebend3r/micro